  `Fs ≤ 2X + 2R`, one has `Fs ≤ C₀(C_coarse) X`: the factors `16/h²` and `h²` cancel exactly
  (`two_level_bound`, `TwoLevelBookkeeping`, `two_level_bound_of_bookkeeping`). With
  `C_coarse = 9/10` and `h = 2^M` this gives `Fs(M) ≤ 2997.2 · X(M)` for every `M ≥ 1`
  (`two_level_bound_uniform`); the bound is attained at `M = 1`
  (`ChainExample.example_two_level_uniform`).
* **One-dimensional arithmetic** (Part 11). For `h > 0`, `2(2h+1)/((h+1)(h+2)) ≤ 4/h` and
  `(2h+1)/(h²+h+1) ≤ 2/h`, so a product of two such values is at most `16/h²`
  (`twoHat_dual_le`, `threeHat_dual_le`, `dualNormSq_le`, `transfer_norm_le`), with the
  determinant and inverse identities that produce these values (`twoHat_gram_det`,
  `twoHat_gram_inv`, `threeHat_det`, `threeHat_schur`, `threeHat_gram_inv`);
  `((2h²+1)/(3h))² ≤ (9/16) h²` for `h ≥ 2`, with equality at `h = 2` and failure at `h = 1`
  (`tent_overlap_le`, `tent_overlap_two`, `tent_overlap_fails_one`); two weighted
  Cauchy–Schwarz (Schur) tests, and `∑_p m_p a_p² ≤ N ∑_p a_p²` when every `m_p ≤ N`
  (`schur_test`, `schur_test_product`, `mult_energy_le`).
* **The Lions-type step** (Part 11). A stable decomposition `d = ∑_x d_x` with `d_x ∈ F_x` and
  `∑_x ‖d_x‖² ≤ C₀ ‖d‖²` (`StableDecomposition`) gives `‖d‖² ≤ C₀ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²`, with `P_x` the
  orthogonal projection onto `F_x` (`lions_step`, `lions_step_div`); with local inequalities
  `‖P_x d‖² ≤ K q_x(d)` and `C₀ ≥ 0` it gives `‖d‖² ≤ C₀ K ∑_x q_x(d)` (`lions_step_local`); and
  with `B ≤ m Q` and `m, C₀ > 0` it gives `B(d)/(m C₀) ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²` (`c0_lower_bound`). In
  finite dimension, `c ‖d‖² ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²` with `c > 0` gives a stable decomposition with
  constant `1/c` (additive Schwarz), so a stable decomposition with some `C₀ > 0` exists iff such
  a Poincaré inequality holds with some `c > 0` (`stableDecomposition_of_poincare`, `lions_iff`,
  `coarse_splitting`). A stable decomposition on the curvature side transports to the fibres
  through a linear isometry (`stableDecomposition_of_isometry`).
* **The aligned-cover Poincaré inequality** (Part 11). Let `L` be a linear isometry, let the
  curvature-side two-level decomposition hold at `h ≥ 2` with `C_coarse ≥ 0` (a hypothesis), and
  let `B ≤ 9 Q`. Then the fibres have a stable decomposition with constant `C₀(C_coarse)`, and
  `‖d‖²/C₀ ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²` and `B(d)/(9 C₀) ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²` (`aligned_cover_poincare`). If these
  hypotheses hold at every depth `M ≥ 1`, with `h = 2^M`, `C_coarse = 9/10` and `0 ≤ B ≤ 9 Q`,
  then `‖d‖²/2997.2 ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²`, `(5/134874) B(d) ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²` and
  `B(d)/26975 ≤ ∑_x ‖P_x d‖²` at every depth, with the same constants
  (`aligned_cover_poincare_uniform`).
* **Variance tensorisation in the first chaos** (Part 11). If `P² = P` and `P C = C Pᵀ`, then
  `P C (1 − P)ᵀ = 0` and `C = P C Pᵀ + (1 − P) C (1 − P)ᵀ` (`cov_cross_zero`, `cov_split`,
  `variance_split`). The `Q`-orthogonal projection `Z (ZᵀQZ)⁻¹ ZᵀQ` satisfies both hypotheses
  with `C = Q⁻¹`, for symmetric invertible `Q` and invertible `ZᵀQZ` (`qProj`, `qProj_idem`,
  `qProj_cov_symm`). For symmetric `C` with `Q C = 1` and `P_x C = C P_xᵀ`, the Poincaré
  inequality `c vᵀQv ≤ ∑_x (P_x v)ᵀ Q (P_x v)` gives `c aᵀCa ≤ ∑_x aᵀ P_x C P_xᵀ a` for every `a`
  (`first_chaos_tensorisation`, `variance_tensorisation_first_chaos`). For `n ≥ 1` and
  `ε ∈ {0, 1}ⁿ`, `(1/n) ∑_i ε_i ≤ 1 − ∏_i (1 − ε_i)` (`chaos_step_scalar`).
* **Surjectivity, ergodicity and codimension across depths** (Part 11). A linear map in which
  every output coordinate has a private input with a nonzero weight is onto, and an iterate of
  onto maps is onto (`surjective_of_private_inputs`, `blockIter`, `blockIter_surjective`). For an
  onto block average, gauge fixing and coarse gauge lifting give `V(S) = S₀` (`section_map_eq`).
  For onto maps that carry each fibre into a coarse fibre, if the fibres span then the coarse
  fibres span; so if the coarse fibres do not span, the fibres do not span at any depth; and, in
  finite dimension, the codimension of the span of the fibres is at least that of the coarse
  fibres (`ergodic_of_ergodic`, `not_ergodic_of_not_ergodic`, `not_ergodic_every_depth`,
  `codim_le`, `codim_fibres_le`, `codim_of_zero_fibres`).
* **The exact depth-0 value** (Part 11). The number `c₀⁽⁰⁾ = (113 − √3769)/270` (`c0Depth0`) is a
  root of `405t² − 339t + 50`, lies in `(0.19114, 0.19115)`, and is not a root of
  `43173t³ − 68175t² + 33264t − 4928`, which is negative for `t ≤ 1/4` (`c0Depth0_root`,
  `c0Depth0_bounds`, `cubic_neg`, `cubic_ne_zero_at_c0Depth0`).
* **Covariance algebra and the law of total covariance** (Part 12). In a real inner-product space
  with a unit vector `1` (the constant function), with `Cov(x, y) = ⟪x, y⟫ − ⟪1, x⟫⟪1, y⟫` and
  `sd x = ‖x − ⟪1, x⟫ 1‖`, conditional expectation is modelled by the orthogonal projection `P_K`
  onto a subspace `K` containing `1`. Then
  `Cov(F, G) = ⟪F − P_K F, G − P_K G⟫ + Cov(P_K F, P_K G)` (`cov_total`, the law of total
  covariance (A1)) and `sd(P_K x) ≤ sd x` (`sd_proj_le`); if a linear isometric equivalence `Θ`
  maps `K` onto `K'`, then `P_{K'} Θ = Θ P_K` (`starProjection_reflect`). For `L²(μ)` and a
  sub-σ-algebra `m`, Mathlib's `condExpL2` is, by definition, the orthogonal projection onto the
  `m`-measurable classes (`condExpL2_eq_starProjection`).
* **Symmetric positive operators** (Part 12). For `T` symmetric with `⟪x, T x⟫ ≥ 0` and
  `T e = λ e`: `λⁿ ⟪e, f⟫² ≤ ⟪f, Tⁿ f⟫ ‖e‖²` (`eigen_mass_le`), so `⟪f, Tᵗ f⟫ ≤ C e^{−mt}` for all
  `t` forces `λ ≤ e^{−m}` whenever `⟪e, f⟫ ≠ 0` (`eigenvalue_le_of_decay`); in finite dimensions
  the same decay of `⟪v, Tᵗ v⟫` gives `‖Tˢ v‖ ≤ e^{−ms} ‖v‖` (`norm_pow_le_of_decay`,
  `norm_pow_le_of_eigen_bound`). A norm gap `‖T v‖ ≤ q ‖v‖` on `Ω^⊥` gives `|λ| ≤ q` for every
  eigenvector `e ≠ 0` in `Ω^⊥`, for any linear `T` (`eigen_le_of_gap`); conversely, in finite
  dimensions, for `T` symmetric with `⟪x, T x⟫ ≥ 0`, `TΩ = Ω`, `‖Ω‖ = 1` and `q ≥ 0`, the
  eigenvalue bound `λ ≤ q` on `Ω^⊥` gives the norm gap (`gap_of_eigen`). Even powers of a
  symmetric operator are positive (`inner_even_pow_nonneg`).
* **Lemma IR** (Part 12). Take a positive-time subspace `P ∋ 1`, linear isometries `Θ` and `τ_s`
  (`s ∈ ℕ`) fixing `1`, a linear map `Ψ : E → ℋ` with `Ψ 1 = Ω`, and an operator `T` on `ℋ` with
  the OS identity `⟪Ψ A, Tˢ Ψ B⟫ = ⟪Θ A, τ_s B⟫` for `A, B ∈ P`. If `‖1‖ = 1`, `T` is symmetric,
  `TΩ = Ω` and `‖T v‖ ≤ e^{−g} ‖v‖` on `Ω^⊥`, then `|Cov(X, Y)| ≤ e^{−gs} sd X sd Y` for all
  `X ∈ Θ(P)` and `Y ∈ τ_s(P)` (`lemmaIR_a_core`, `lemmaIR_a`). Conversely, if `ℋ` is
  finite-dimensional, `Ψ(P) = ℋ`, `T` is symmetric with `⟪v, T v⟫ ≥ 0`, and
  `|Cov(Θ A, τ_s A)| ≤ C e^{−gs} sd(Θ A) sd(τ_s A)` for all `A ∈ P` and `s`, with any constant
  `C`, then the gap holds (`lemmaIR_b`); together they give an equivalence (`lemmaIR_iff`).
* **Clustering** (Part 12). For `T` symmetric with `⟪x, T x⟫ ≥ 0`, `TΩ = Ω`, `‖Ω‖ = 1` and a
  spanning set `S` whose truncated correlations decay, `⟪f, Tᵗ f⟫ − ⟪Ω, f⟫² ≤ C_f e^{−mt}`, every
  `e` with `T e = λ e` and `λ > e^{−m}` is a multiple of `Ω` (`clustering_eigvec`). In finite
  dimensions and for `m > 0`, `σ(T) ⊆ {1} ∪ [0, e^{−m}]` and the eigenspace of `1` is `ℝ Ω`
  (`clustering_spectrum`), and every `λ ∈ σ(T)` with `λ > 0` has `−log λ = 0` or `−log λ ≥ m`
  (`hamiltonian_spectrum`).
* **Theorem MIX** (Part 12). Core inequality: for subspaces `B`, `B_p`, `B_f` containing `1`,
  `sd F, sd G ≤ σ`, the UV bound `|⟪F − P_B F, G − P_B G⟫| ≤ ε_UV`, the localisation bounds
  `‖P_B F − P_{B_p} F‖, ‖P_B G − P_{B_f} G‖ ≤ ε_loc` and the mixing bound
  `|Cov(x, y)| ≤ ρ sd x sd y` on `B_p × B_f`, `|Cov(F, G)| ≤ ε_UV + ρσ² + 2σ ε_loc` (`mix_core`).
  Assembled, in filtration form (a block subspace `B`, filtrations `Fut c` and `Past c`, `c ∈ ℤ`,
  exchanged by a reflection `Θ` and moved by coarse shifts, observables `O` and `G t`, `L > 0`,
  `a > 0`): from the UV bound `≤ C_UV e^{−m₁ t a}` for `t a ≥ τ_UV`, localisation errors
  `≤ C_loc e^{−κ r}` at coarse distance `r`, the variance bound `sd² ≤ V`, and the mixing bound
  `|Cov(x, y)| ≤ C_IR e^{−gs} sd x sd y` between `Past (−1)` and `Fut s`, it follows that
  `|Cov(Θ O, G t)| ≤ (C_UV + C_IR V e^{μ} + 2√V C_loc e^{κ+μ}) e^{−min(m₁, μ/(L a)) t a}` for
  `t a ≥ τ_UV`, with `μ = gκ/(2g + κ)` (`mix_of_mixing`; the rate step is `mix_rate`). The
  mixing bound may be replaced by the gap of a coarse transfer operator with an OS identity on
  `Fut 0`, through Lemma IR (a), with `C_IR = 1` (`mix_of_gap`). The constant is at most the
  cruder `C_UV + (C_IR V + 2√V C_loc) e^{κ+μ}` (`mix_const_le`).

Part 10 ends with examples on concrete data that apply its main theorems, and with witnesses for the
two bounds that the examples do not apply (`witness_exp_sq_mul_kappaLead_le`,
`witness_treeConst_four_le_128`); each docstring says which hypotheses it lists and which of its
instances are degenerate (on the tree-gauge data, `Q` and `D` are constant on the admissible set).

Part 11 ends with examples on concrete data in the namespaces `ArithExample`, `ChainExample`,
`SchurExample`, `LionsExample`, `CovExample`, `SectionExample` and `ChaosExample`, and with
witnesses for the lemmas that the examples do not apply (`WitnessExample`); `lions_iff` has no
hypotheses besides its type-class binders. Each docstring says which hypotheses it lists and which
of its instances are degenerate (a depth family constant in the depth, the identity isometry, local
forms equal to the global norm, bookkeeping met with a wide margin).

Part 12 ends with examples on concrete data: a toy OS structure on `ℝ³` with `T = diag(1, 1/2)` on
`ℝ²`, to which Lemma IR, the clustering results and the core inequality of Theorem MIX are applied
(in the last, the IR term carries the whole correlation), and the assembled Theorem MIX on a trivial
block algebra, where the UV term carries the whole correlation. It adds a witness on concrete data
for each theorem that the examples do not apply (`witness_*`); the one for
`condExpL2_eq_starProjection` uses the uniform probability measure on `Bool × Bool`.

## Scope

Parts 10 and 11 are results about proof methods at Gaussian order (the one-shot loss in the tree
gauge, and an aligned-cover Poincaré inequality in two dimensions); Part 12 is an abstract statement
about one proof architecture, whose lattice and measure inputs enter only as hypotheses. Nothing
here is about the Yang–Mills measure. The estimates (D) (the cube estimate `D(n, α)`) and (E) (the
existence of a nontrivial continuum limit satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms with a gap in
physical units) remain open, and so does the mass gap; nothing here addresses them. The
four-dimensional aligned-cover case is not addressed.

## Not formalised here

* in Part 10: the weighted energy estimate itself (the Fourier input); the lattice inputs (the
  incidence counts `4` and `6`, the Lipschitz property of the weights, the shell count of the
  weight sum); the minimality of the tree column on the tree section and the gauge-fixing
  property of that section; the tree-gauge `ℓ¹` inequality and the local flux identities behind
  its telescoped form (fillings, tree paths, box multiplicities, the contraction of the curvature
  under block averaging): all of these enter only as hypotheses. Also not formalised: the
  tightened local constants `c₂ ≤ 31/16` and `c₁ c_B ≤ 4` (assumed in `treeConst_four_le_128`);
  the identification of `Σ₀ ∩ ker A ∩ ker Q` with the pure gauges (not used); the exact excess
  factor `1 + b²/24 + O(b⁴)` of the weight sum, of which only the majorant `e^{4b}` is proved;
  and the corrected constant `κ_*`, which is not defined.
* in Part 11: the lattice model (the section of link fields, the aligned cover by sixteen boxes,
  the fibres and their supports, the supports of the block averages, the gauge correctors inside
  an aligned box, the description of the curvature fibres by coarse data) and the isometry
  property of the lattice curvature map, which enter only as hypotheses (`L` is assumed to be a
  linear isometry); the two-level decomposition itself, which enters through its bookkeeping
  inequalities; the reading of the one-dimensional rational functions as Gram entries, dual
  norms, transfer norms and tent overlaps; the multiplicity bound `m_p ≤ 9` and the exact finite
  computation `C_coarse = 9/10`, which enter as a hypothesis and a parameter; the identification
  of `c₀⁽⁰⁾` with the splitting constant of the depth-0 cover; the Gaussian identification
  `E[Var(aᵀφ | 𝒢_x)] = aᵀ P_x C P_xᵀ a` and the higher-chaos decomposition (only the scalar
  joint-eigenvector step is proved); and the bracket `c_VT ≥ 4 c₀`, which rests on a numerically
  observed multiplicity bound `m_p ≥ 4`. The four-dimensional case is not addressed.
* in Part 12: the probabilistic and lattice inputs of Theorem MIX (the UV bound, the
  localisation bounds, the IR hypothesis in either form, and the variance bound), which enter
  only as hypotheses; the identification of conditional expectations with the orthogonal
  projections used here, beyond the definition of Mathlib's `condExpL2` (in particular, that the
  constants lie in the subspace of measurable classes); the Osterwalder–Schrader reconstruction
  of `ℋ`, `Ψ` and `T` from reflection positivity, which enters only through the OS identity; the
  block maps of the lattice application, which the filtration form replaces; continuum limits;
  Lemma IR (b) and the spectral form of clustering in infinite dimensions (they are proved for
  finite-dimensional `ℋ` only), and the Hamiltonian `−log T` as an operator; and a
  finite-dimensional instance of the assembled Theorem MIX with a non-zero IR term.

## Conventions

* Labels such as Lemma IR, Theorem MIX, (A1), H-UV, H-loc, H-IR and S1 name the mathematical
  statements that the parts formalise or assume; each is stated where it is used. Nothing from Parts
  1–9 is imported.
